@page
@model Parley.Web.Pages.Projects.RegisterModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = $"Register an agent in {Model.Project}";
}

<h1>Register an agent in @Model.Project</h1>

<p>
    An agent's name comes from the configuration its server is launched with, and from nowhere
    else. No call can supply it, which is what makes "a sender cannot forge the agent it claims to
    be" a property of the design rather than good behaviour. So this page tells you what to write;
    it cannot register anything on your behalf.
</p>

<h2>1. Name the agent in the repository</h2>

<p>Put this in <code>.mcp.json</code> at the root of the repository that will act as the agent:</p>

<pre><code>{
  "mcpServers": {
    "parley-@Model.Project": {
      "command": "parley-mcp",
      "env": {
        "PARLEY_AGENT": "&lt;a short name, lower case&gt;",
        "PARLEY_OWNS": "&lt;one sentence: what this repository owns, and what it does not&gt;",
        "PARLEY_PROJECT": "@Model.Project"
      }
    }
  }
}</code></pre>

<p>
    The key is <code>parley-</code> and this project's name. A repository may answer in more than one
    project by holding one entry for each, under its own key and with its own <code>owns</code> line;
    brokers do not federate, so the project an entry names is the only set of peers it can reach. An
    entry already there under the bare <code>parley</code> key belongs to a deployment that names no
    projects and should be left as it is.
</p>

<p>
    <code>PARLEY_PROJECT</code> names the project and the machine's own manifest turns that into an
    address, which is why it survives the project moving to another port. <b>This page used to show
    <code>PARLEY_BROKER</code> here instead</b>, and an address is the right answer in exactly one
    case: the repository is on a machine whose manifest does not name this project, so there is
    nothing to resolve the name against. Use <code>"PARLEY_BROKER": "@Model.Endpoint"</code> in place
    of <code>PARLEY_PROJECT</code> there — and note that enrolling such a repository later, with
    <code>parley enroll</code> or from the terminal, treats an address-pinned entry as belonging to
    whichever project holds that address and writes this project's entry beside it rather than
    through it.
</p>

<p>
    The <code>owns</code> line is read by every other agent when it decides who to ask, so it is
    worth writing as a boundary rather than as a description. Say what the repository does not own
    as well as what it does.
</p>

<p>
    No credential belongs in that file. Where one is needed it is a reference —
    <code>env:</code>, <code>keychain:</code> or <code>file:</code> — which the broker resolves at
    invocation and never stores. That is what makes the configuration safe to commit.
</p>

<p>
    That file is usually tracked, so <strong>which branch is checked out decides whether the
    agent exists</strong>. Written on a feature branch, it is there while that branch is out and
    gone on any branch that predates it — and nothing reports it: the session starts, the tools
    are absent, and a peer's question waits out its window and is ghosted. Put the entry on the
    branch everything else is cut from.
</p>

<h2>2. Open the repository</h2>

<p>
    Start a session in that repository. The server attaches when it launches, and attaching is
    what registers the agent — there is no separate step, and no command to run afterwards.
</p>

<h2>3. Confirm it worked</h2>

<p>
    Come back to <a href="/Projects/Agents?project=@Model.Project">this project's agents</a>. The
    new name appears there once its session has attached. If it does not, the server did not
    start: the session's own output says why, and the usual cause is that the repository was
    already open when the file was written.
</p>
